  /* ===================== Responsive ===================== */
  @media (max-width: 48rem) {
    .tmf__title {
      font-size: clamp(1.375rem, 6.4vw, 1.75rem);
    }
  }

  /* ===================== The scatter =====================
     The frame's own layout, and no scene: a tall stage, the title stuck in
     the middle of it, the tiles at their measured positions. The scroll does
     all of the work — the only thing the script touches here is opacity.

     Nothing in this block may introduce `overflow: hidden` on an ancestor of
     the title. That is what would break the sticky, and every tile is inside
     the stage's width anyway, so there is nothing to clip. */
  /* --h is written on the element: the lead plus the scatter, both of which
     the component owns because it also has to place the tiles inside them. */
  :global(html[data-js='true']) .tmf__stage {
    height: var(--h);
    padding: 0;
  }

  /* Half a screen down in flow and stuck half a screen from the top, which
     are the same place: the title is centred from the first pixel of the
     section and stays centred until the stage runs out from under it. The
     translate is what centres it on that line rather than hanging it from
     it — a transform, so it costs the sticky nothing. */
  :global(html[data-js='true']) .tmf__title {
    position: sticky;
    top: 50svh;
    /* A ZERO-HEIGHT BOX ON THE MIDDLE LINE, with the words overflowing it
       evenly above and below — and that is what keeps it centred to the end.

       It was a box as tall as its lines, stuck with its top on the middle and
       lifted half its own height by a translate. A sticky box stops when its
       margin box meets the foot of its container, so that one stopped with the
       stage's foot one title-height under the middle — and then rode the
       stage's foot up the screen. Measured at the end of the section, the
       heading sat 160px off the bottom of the cream with the footer coming up
       under it, which is the report.

       With no height there is nothing of the box below the middle line, and the
       bottom margin is exactly half a screen: it stops at the one moment the
       stage's foot reaches the bottom of the window. The title is centred in
       the section's last full screen, and from there the two leave together.

       NOT A FLEX CONTAINER, which is what this first was, and it broke the
       title into one word a line. SplitText wraps every word in an element
       before it measures where the lines fall, and every child of a flex
       container is blockified into an item of its own — so in a column the
       words stacked one per row, and SplitText faithfully cut ten lines out
       of a two-line heading. Measured at 1440: ten lines, each the full 711px.
       The words live in `.tmf__title-text` instead, an ordinary block that is
       lifted half its own height below. */
    height: 0;
    /* UNDER the tiles. The tiles are the testimonials and the line is what
       they are passing over, so a tile covers the words rather than the words
       being printed across the tile.

       It used to be the other way up, drawn over everything in
       `mix-blend-mode: difference` so it could never be covered — an x-ray
       that inverted whatever it crossed. That is gone with it: under the tiles
       there is only the cream ground behind the line, so it is plain ink.
       What marks a tile crossing it now is the tile's own shadow — see
       `.tmf__film[data-over-title]` below and initTitleShadows in
       src/scripts/testimonialsFloat.ts. */
    z-index: 1;
    max-width: min(44.4375rem, calc(100% - 2 * var(--section-padding-x)));
    margin: 50svh auto;
    color: var(--color-ink);

    /* A line lying over the tiles would otherwise take their presses: it spans
       the middle of the screen and the films underneath are the only controls
       this section has. */
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* The words, hanging off the heading's zero-height line and lifted by half
     their own height so they are centred on it. A percentage translate is of
     the element's own box, so this needs no figure for how tall the title is —
     two lines at 1440, three or four on a phone. */
  :global(html[data-js='true']) .tmf__title-text {
    display: block;
    translate: 0 -50%;
  }

  /* Over the title now. The list covers the whole stage and has nothing to be
     pressed, so it hands pointer events back; only the tiles take them. */
  :global(html[data-js='true']) .tmf__films {
    display: block;
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 2;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  :global(html[data-js='true']) .tmf__film {
    position: absolute;
    left: var(--l);
    top: var(--t);
    width: var(--w);
    aspect-ratio: var(--ar);
    /* The layer above sets `pointer-events: none` so the title underneath stays
       selectable between the tiles; each tile takes it back for itself. */
    pointer-events: auto;
    transition: box-shadow 0.45s var(--ease-out-expo);
  }

  /* LIFTED OFF THE LINE, and only while it is over it.

     The script sets this for exactly as long as the tile's box overlaps the
     painted words — not the heading's box, which runs the full measure on a
     line that may be half as long — so a tile passing beside the title in the
     same band of the screen does not pick it up. It is the only thing that
     says one is above the other now that the line no longer inverts through
     the tiles, and it goes again the moment the words are clear.

     Two layers, a wide soft one for the lift and a tight one under the edge so
     the tile does not read as floating in fog. Drawn on the tile's own box,
     which a film's `overflow: hidden` does not clip — that clips its contents,
     not its shadow. */
  :global(html[data-js='true']) .tmf__film[data-over-title] {
    box-shadow:
      0 28px 64px -18px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.32),
      0 6px 18px -8px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.2);
  }

  /* And a card is still as tall as its own words here.

     This has to be repeated rather than left to the rule further up, because
     that one is a bare class and this is a class behind an attribute selector
     on <html> — higher specificity, later in the file, and it was winning.
     Measured: every card came out 278px tall on 126px of content, all three
     identical, which is exactly what a shared aspect ratio looks like. */
  :global(html[data-js='true']) .tmf__film--card {
    aspect-ratio: auto;
    pointer-events: auto;
  }

  /* A PHONE: A COLUMN WITH ONE GAP, not six measured positions.

     The frame's scatter places every tile at a percentage of the stage's
     height, which only works while a tile's height is known in advance — and a
     card's is not: it is as tall as its words, and on a phone the words are
     most of the width. So the spots that had room for a card at 1440 did not
     have it at 390. Two faults came out of that, both reported:

       - Cards were given a FILM'S aspect ratio. The card rule and the film rule
         had the same specificity here and the film rule came second, so a card
         was locked to a shape and its logo ran out of the bottom of it.
       - The gaps were whatever the percentages left, so they varied from tile
         to tile and a card could come down over the film below it.

     Here the tiles are in flow, one under the other, with a single gap. The
     scatter survives sideways: each tile keeps the frame's own left offset and
     width for a phone, so the column still zig-zags. The stage is as tall as
     its tiles; `--nt` and `--nh` are no longer read at this width.

     The title still sits in the middle of the screen and under the tiles. Its
     half-screen bottom margin now puts the first tile's top at the foot of the
     window as the title settles, and the half screen of padding under the
     last tile is what lets that tile finish passing over the title before the
     title lets go. */
  @media (max-width: 48rem) {
    :global(html[data-js='true']) .tmf__stage {
      height: auto;
    }

    :global(html[data-js='true']) .tmf__films {
      position: relative;
      inset: auto;
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: clamp(2rem, 9vw, 3rem);
      padding-bottom: 50svh;
    }

    :global(html[data-js='true']) .tmf__film {
      position: relative;
      left: auto;
      top: auto;
      width: var(--nw);
      margin-left: var(--nl);
      aspect-ratio: var(--nar);
    }

    /* Two classes, so it outranks the film rule above whatever the order. */
    :global(html[data-js='true']) .tmf__film.tmf__film--card {
      aspect-ratio: auto;
    }
  }

  /* The tiles need no pre-state: they begin a screen below the fold and the
     script scales them from 0.8 as they come up, so there is nothing to hide.
     The title does — SplitText masks its lines, and this covers the gap until
     it has run. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    :global(html[data-js='true']) .tmf__title {
      opacity: 0;
    }
  }

  /* The shadow still comes and goes — it says what is on top of what, which is
     content — but it arrives rather than fading in. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    :global(html[data-js='true']) .tmf__film {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  /* ===================== Not the picked cut =====================
     The wall (Testimonials.astro) is the other option on the "Testimonial
     section" axis, and the default. Stated this way round — shown only when
     this one is explicitly chosen — so that with no JS at all, and therefore
     no `data-var-*` on <html>, exactly one of the two stands. */
  .tmf {
    display: none;
  }

  /* `flow-root`, not `block`, and it is load-bearing.

     The title is placed half a screen down with a top margin, and a top margin
     on a first child COLLAPSES OUT of its parents — through the stage and
     through the section — so it stopped being space inside this section and
     became space above it. Measured: the client band ended at 16366 and this
     section's own box did not begin until 16816, with 450px of the page's own
     white between them and the title floating in the middle of it.

     A block formatting context contains that margin. The section's box now
     starts where the band ends, the half screen is inside it, and the ground
     is painted under it. Nothing moves: the same 450px is in the same place,
     it is just this section's now.

     It has to be stated HERE rather than on the `.tmf` rule above, which is
     where it was first put. That rule sets `display: none` — this cut is
     hidden unless it is the one chosen — so a `display` beside the background
     is a declaration that never survives to the page. */
  :global(html[data-var-testimonials='float']) .tmf {
    display: flow-root;
  }
</style>
